From f40148d7abb06f21a16298c2ed4a9882b26b079e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:28:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 22/44] feat(chexc): sections 5-6 conformance -- effects on
 their own axis, unconditional clauses

The revision that moved effect requirements into the base design also settled several
rules the prototype had guessed at. Audited section by section, by probing:

- The clause grammar is unconditional. Every clause position is a parse error in the
  language without clauses, so recognizing the soft keywords there changes no valid
  program; what the feature flag decides is what a clause *means*. With checking off the
  clauses parse and impose no obligations.

- Effect requirements follow the effect-handler feature, not the exception-checking level:
  the capability pass now runs when either is enabled, reports a missing handler as an
  error at every exception level -- an unhandled 'perform' fails at run time, so it has no
  migration level -- and reports exception misses only when checking is on. Inference is
  skipped entirely when checking is off, since effect requirements are never inferred.

- A 'captures' clause may name commands: an instance captures the handler capabilities its
  methods need exactly as it captures exception ones.

- A type parameter whose own bound is unchecked is rejected in a clause. A type parameter
  is otherwise admitted -- a requirement that instantiates to an unchecked type is
  trivially satisfied -- but when the bound is already unchecked, every instantiation is.

- Assumption imports are package-scoped: one annotated import binds every call into that
  dependency from the whole consuming package, whatever file wrote it. The map is no
  longer keyed by file.

- '@AssumeThrows[]' is rejected with a message of its own, and the '...' marker is rejected
  on a 'performs' clause: effect lists are always written, so there is nothing to open.

Effective visibility for inference eligibility was implemented and reverted in the same
round: it makes an internal class's public override inference-eligible, and an inferred
override is not re-checked against the declaration it overrides -- the machinery the
static-member rule already waits on.

